# ComposeUX — The Bindable-Order / Visibility Container [SPEC:CXC-ORDER]

> **This is a CAPTURE of built code, not a design.** `OrderedFlowContainer` (`type: ordered_stack`) is a `FlexContainer` subclass that overrides ONE hook — `_flow_children()`, the "children to LAY OUT, in layout order" seam every flex/grid container iterates instead of `self.children` — and binds that hook's answer to ComposeBus state. Publish a list of `widget_id`s and the children reorder; publish `{order, hidden, visible}` and they reorder AND hide; publish on the separate `visibility_namespace` and only the shown-set moves. The container REFLOWS: a hidden child leaves the FLOW entirely (it contributes no measured size, is not drawn, is not hit-tested), which is the property no `visible:` attribute binding has and the reason [CXC-ICL-PANE-SWAP] rides this container rather than a `visible:` binding. Everything is REVERSIBLE because `self.children` is never mutated: draw z-order and hit-test still walk the declared list, and clearing the state restores the original layout. With NO state published the container is byte-for-byte a `stack` (the identity path returns the declared list unchanged), so converting a `stack` to an `ordered_stack` is a zero-diff change until a state drives it — the backward-compatibility property that made the console rail conversion safe. It was commissioned as workstream W2 ("bindable-order container + hot-add + S4 reflow") of [DEC-2026-07-25-FOLLOWUP-CALLS-SETTLED] and landed in commit `65ad7fc`; the decision commissioned the CODE and no spec was ever written. Seven references across the corpus cite `[SPEC:CXC-ORDER]` and, until this document, none of them resolved. Every claim below is traced to a code line, and where covered, to one of the 9 tests in `python/tests/render/test_ordered_container.py`. Where the class docstring (the de-facto spec) and the actual behaviour DISAGREE, the code wins and the disagreement is recorded in [CXC-ORD-OPEN].

---

## 1. Intent & capture discipline

### [CXC-ORD-WHY] Why this document exists — the primitive was built, cited seven times, and never specified

- definition: the reusable primitive under any *customizable-layout* surface — a rearrangeable dashboard, an Intelligence-drivable collaboration region, a user-hidable card rail, a pane swap. It answers ONE question the base flex container cannot: *which children lay out, and in what order*, driven by the bus at runtime, reversibly. It is implemented in `python/composeio/render/engine/layout.py:347-517` as `OrderedFlowContainer(FlexContainer)`, constructed by the parser at `python/composeio/render/engine/parser.py:1517-1535`, and used in production by `intelligence/console/console.compose.yaml:1517-1523`.
- capture-discipline (load-bearing, read this before editing this document): **the code is the truth.** This document was authored AFTER the fact from the implementation and its tests. It specifies nothing that is not already built. A reader who finds behaviour here that they would have designed differently should look in [CXC-ORD-OPEN] — the disagreement is recorded there — and MUST NOT "fix" it by editing the prose, because a spec that quietly re-designs makes the spec and the code disagree SILENTLY, which is strictly worse than the seven dangling references this document closes.
- provenance: commissioned as workstream **W2 — "bindable-order container + hot-add + S4 reflow"** (D1 hot-add + D2 S4 widget-selection, "one converged effort") of [DEC-2026-07-25-FOLLOWUP-CALLS-SETTLED]; landed in commit `65ad7fc`. The decision commissioned the CODE; the spec was never authored. That gap is what this document closes — the gap [CXC-ICL-PANE-SWAP] named as a finding ("its SPEC is a docstring. A builder following the reference will find nothing").
- role: the resolvable target for every `[SPEC:CXC-ORDER]` citation in the corpus and in the source — `layout.py:144`, `layout.py:344`, `layout.py:349`, `parser.py:1518`, `console.compose.yaml:1019`, `console.compose.yaml:1507`, `test_ordered_container.py:2`, `drive_ordered_reflow.py:2`, and the `uses:`/`USES` edges carried by [CXC-ICL-PANE-SWAP] and [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH].
- reuse-by-reference: the layout algebra is the existing `FlexContainer` ([CXC-R-ENGINE-LAYOUT]); the draw + hit-test skip of an invisible child is the existing base `Container` (`layout.py:201-203`, `:214-216`); the damage model is the existing scoped `request_redraw()` (`python/composeio/render/widget.py:169-180`); the hot-add re-bind is the existing `RenderApp.add_widget_live` (`runtime.py:907-973`). This primitive forks NONE of them — it overrides ONE method.
- see-also: [CXC-ORD-CONTAINER], [CXC-ORD-OPEN], [CXC-ICL-PANE-SWAP]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ORDER]

---

## 2. The primitive as built

### [CXC-ORD-CONTAINER] `OrderedFlowContainer` — a `FlexContainer` that overrides the `_flow_children()` layout-order hook, and nothing else

- definition: `class OrderedFlowContainer(FlexContainer)` (`layout.py:347`). The base `Container._flow_children()` (`layout.py:137-147`) returns `self.children` and is documented as "the children to LAY OUT, in layout order" — every container that lays out by list order iterates THAT, not `self.children` directly, so a subclass can reorder or exclude children from layout WITHOUT mutating the tree. `FlexContainer.measure` (`:262`) and `FlexContainer._arrange_children` (`:282`) both call it. `OrderedFlowContainer` overrides it (`:402-421`) and adds bus-facing state — it overrides no other layout, draw, or hit-test method.
- state it carries (`layout.py:386-393`): `widget_type = "ordered_stack"` (assigned AFTER `FlexContainer.__init__` set `"stack"`); `state_namespace: str` and `visibility_namespace: str` (both default `""` = unbound); `_order: Optional[List[str]]` (`None` = declared order); `_hidden: set` (subtractive); `_visible_only: Optional[set]` (an allow-list; `None` = inactive).
- the shown predicate (`layout.py:397-400`): `_shown(wid)` is `wid in self._visible_only` when an allow-list is active, else `wid not in self._hidden`. The allow-list therefore DOMINATES the hidden set whenever both are somehow set.
- the layout-order computation (`layout.py:402-421`), stated so a reimplementer does not guess: (1) if `_order is None` AND `_hidden` is empty AND `_visible_only is None`, return `self.children` unchanged — the zero-cost identity path ([CXC-ORD-SUPERSET]); (2) build an id→child map with `setdefault` (FIRST child wins on a duplicate id, `:408`); (3) if there is no `_order`, return the declared list filtered by `_shown`; (4) otherwise emit, for each id in `_order`, the mapped child if it exists, is unseen, and is shown; then (5) append every remaining declared child that is unseen and shown, IN DECLARED ORDER. An id in `_order` naming no child is silently ignored; a child named by no id keeps declared order, after the ordered ones.
- requirement: a from-spec reimplementation MUST express order and visibility ONLY through the `_flow_children()` hook (plus the `visible` flag, [CXC-ORD-REVERSIBLE]); it MUST NOT reorder or remove entries from `self.children`, MUST NOT override `draw`/`hit_test_tree`/`measure`, and MUST preserve the identity path as an object-identity return of the declared list (the zero-diff guarantee is asserted at the PIXEL level, not merely the order level).
- default: `direction="column"`, `align="start"`, `justify="start"`, both namespaces `""`, `_order=None`, `_hidden=set()`, `_visible_only=None` (`layout.py:380-393`). **NOTE the `direction` default is `column`, whereas `FlexContainer`/`stack` defaults to `row`** — see [CXC-ORD-OPEN] O1; this is the one place the "drop-in superset" claim is qualified.
- scenario:
  - when: an `ordered_stack` is constructed and nothing is ever published to its namespaces
  - then: `_flow_children()` returns the declared child list object itself, `measure`/`_arrange_children` behave exactly as `FlexContainer`, and no child's `visible` flag is ever written
- see-also: [CXC-ORD-SUPERSET], [CXC-ORD-ORDER-STATE], [CXC-R-ENGINE-LAYOUT]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ORDER]

### [CXC-ORD-SUPERSET] INVARIANT — a strict ADDITIVE SUPERSET of `stack`: zero-diff until a state publishes

- definition: with no state published the container lays out **byte-for-byte** like the `FlexContainer` it subclasses, because `_flow_children()` short-circuits to the declared list (`layout.py:404-405`). Converting an existing `stack` to an `ordered_stack` therefore changes NOTHING on glass until a publish actually drives it — which is why the console's four-card rail could be converted in place (`console.compose.yaml:1507-1523`, "this conversion is a zero-diff default; only an S4 publish changes a pixel").
- requirement: the identity path MUST remain a genuine no-op — same layout order, same measured sizes, same arranged rects, same pixels — and MUST NOT touch any child's `visible` flag. A reimplementation that (for example) always rebuilt a filtered list, or eagerly ran `_sync_visibility()` on mount, would break this invariant even while producing the same order, because the guarantee is asserted against a rendered `stack` at `tolerance=0, max_diff_ratio=0.0`.
- invariant-rationale (why this is load-bearing, not a nicety): it makes adoption of the primitive RISK-FREE and therefore reversible. Any surface can become bindable-order in one key, and if the driving state is never published the surface is provably unchanged. Every consumer to date ([CXC-ORD-PRODUCTION], [CXC-ICL-PANE-SWAP]) relies on that: the swap adds a container capability to a live production rail without a layout regression to re-verify.
- caveat (stated, not corrected): the guarantee is byte-identical for a conversion that carries an EXPLICIT `direction:` (as the console rail and every test do). Converting a `stack` that relied on the implicit `row` default yields a `column` layout, because the two constructors' `direction` defaults differ ([CXC-ORD-OPEN] O1).
- verified-by: `test_ordered_container.py::test_default_layout_is_byte_identical_to_stack` (`:78-93`) — renders the same scene as `ordered_stack` and as `stack`, asserts a pixel match at zero tolerance AND that every child's `(x, y, w, h)` coincides exactly.
- scenario:
  - when: a scene declares `type: ordered_stack` with `state_namespace` set, and no producer ever publishes
  - then: the rendered surface is pixel-identical to the same scene declared as `type: stack`, and every child's arranged rect is identical
- see-also: [CXC-ORD-CONTAINER], [CXC-ORD-PRODUCTION], [CXC-ORD-OPEN]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ORDER]

### [CXC-ORD-ORDER-STATE] `state_namespace` — ONE namespace carrying EITHER a pure-reorder list OR an `{order, hidden, visible}` dict

- definition: the container subscribes `state_namespace` (alias key `order_namespace`, `parser.py:1529-1530`) and applies each delivery through `apply_order_state(value)` (`layout.py:433-461`). The payload is one of four shapes, resolved by type:
  - **a LIST / tuple** → PURE REORDER (`:457-458`): the listed ids first in the given order, every unlisted child kept in declared order after them. Visibility is NOT touched — a bare reorder never un-hides anything.
  - **a DICT** → up to three keys, applied in a FIXED clause order: `order` (`:441-444`), then `visible` (`:445-450`), then `hidden` (`:451-456`).
    - `order`: a list/tuple sets the explicit order; any non-list value (including `null`) CLEARS it back to declared order.
    - `visible`: an ALLOW-LIST — only the listed ids are shown; it also clears `_hidden`. The key is IGNORED when its value is `None` (the `value.get("visible") is not None` guard, `:445`); a non-list non-`None` value clears both the allow-list and the hidden set.
    - `hidden`: a SUBTRACTIVE hidden set — the listed ids leave the flow; it also clears the allow-list (`_visible_only = None`). A non-list value (including `null`) clears the hidden set.
  - **`None`** → clears the ORDER only (`:459-460`); the shown-set is retained.
  - **anything else** (a scalar, a string, a number) → no branch matches; every field keeps its current value and the delivery is a silent no-op ([CXC-ORD-OPEN] O8).
- requirement: a reimplementation MUST accept all four shapes with exactly these semantics; MUST keep unlisted children in DECLARED order after the ordered ones (not sorted, not dropped); MUST ignore an order entry naming no child rather than raising; MUST treat `hidden` and `visible` as mutually exclusive with the LAST-APPLIED clause winning (which, given the fixed clause order, means `hidden` wins whenever a dict carries both — see [CXC-ORD-OPEN] O2); and MUST NOT raise on a malformed payload.
- default: unset/never-published → `_order is None` → declared order. The production namespace `_app/console/collab_order` declares NO `initial:` (`console.compose.yaml:1022`), so the console rail boots in declared order.
- verified-by: `test_list_state_reorders` (`:96-104`, list reorder + visibility untouched); `test_dict_state_reorders_and_hides_and_compacts` (`:107-118`, `{order, hidden}` reorders AND hides AND the survivors COMPACT — `c` moves up into `b`'s slot); `test_visible_allowlist_shows_only_listed` (`:138-145`); `test_visibility_is_sticky_until_changed` (`:121-135`, `{hidden: []}` restores).
- scenario:
  - when: `["c", "a", "b"]` is published to a rail declaring `a, b, c`
  - then: the layout order becomes `c, a, b`, all three stay visible, and `self.children` is still `[a, b, c]`
- scenario:
  - when: `{"order": ["a", "b", "c"], "hidden": ["b"]}` is published
  - then: `b.visible` is False, `b` leaves the flow, and `c` moves UP into `b`'s slot — the rail COMPACTS (`yc == ya + CARD_H + GAP`), it does not leave a hole
- scenario:
  - when: `{"hidden": ["b"]}` is published, then a bare `["c", "b", "a"]` reorder is published
  - then: `b` stays hidden — visibility is STICKY across a pure reorder; publishing `{"hidden": []}` restores it
- see-also: [CXC-ORD-SHOWN-SET], [CXC-ORD-VIS-NAMESPACE], [CXC-PS-GRANULARITY]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ORDER]

### [CXC-ORD-SHOWN-SET] The shown-set — a subtractive `hidden` set XOR a `visible` allow-list, reflected onto each child's `visible` flag

- definition: the shown-set is held as `_hidden` (subtractive) XOR `_visible_only` (an allow-list), resolved by `_shown()` (`layout.py:397-400`). Whenever a visibility source is active — or JUST WAS, so a clear can restore — `_commit` calls `_sync_visibility()` (`:423-429`, gated at `:486`), which writes `child.visible = self._shown(child.widget_id)` for EVERY child. A REORDER-ONLY container never touches child visibility at all.
- consequence — the two effects of hiding, which are different mechanisms: (a) the child is excluded from `_flow_children()`, so `FlexContainer.measure` (`:262-272`) and `_arrange_children` (`:282-283`) never see it — it occupies NO space and the container's own measured size shrinks; and (b) its `visible` flag is False, so the base `Container.draw` (`:201-203`) and `hit_test_tree` (`:214-216`) skip it — no pixels, not tappable. Both are required: (a) alone would still draw it at a stale rect; (b) alone is exactly the `visible:`-binding behaviour that leaves a hole.
- requirement: hiding MUST produce both effects; a hidden child MUST NOT contribute measured size, MUST NOT draw, and MUST NOT be hit-tested; clearing the shown-set MUST restore the child to its declared position with no other state carried over.
- caveat (stated, not corrected): a hidden child's own `arranged_rect` is NOT zeroed — it retains the rect from the last layout in which it participated, because `_arrange_children` only assigns to children in the flow. The correct assertions for "it left the flow" are that the child is absent from `_flow_children()` and that the CONTAINER's measured size shrank — not that the child's rect collapsed to zero ([CXC-ORD-OPEN] O6).
- default: no visibility source → `_hidden` empty, `_visible_only` None, every child shown, no `visible` flag ever written.
- verified-by: `test_dict_state_reorders_and_hides_and_compacts` (`:107-118`, the compaction proves the no-space half); `test_visible_allowlist_shows_only_listed` (`:138-145`); `test_visibility_is_sticky_until_changed` (`:121-135`, the restore half).
- scenario:
  - when: `{"visible": ["c", "a"]}` is published to a rail declaring `a, b, c`
  - then: only `a` and `c` are shown, in declared relative order (`["a", "c"]`), and `b.visible` is False
- see-also: [CXC-ORD-VIS-NAMESPACE], [CXC-ORD-REVERSIBLE], [CXC-ORD-OPEN]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ORDER]

### [CXC-ORD-VIS-NAMESPACE] INVARIANT — `visibility_namespace` is a GENUINELY SEPARATE second subscription; order and visibility do not clobber each other

- definition: `visibility_namespace` is a second, independent bus subscription (`parser.py:1534-1535`) routed to `apply_visibility_state(value)` (`layout.py:463-474`), NOT a key inside the order payload. It accepts a LIST of hidden ids, or a `{id: bool}` SHOWN-map (`{k for k, v in value.items() if not _truthy(v)}` → the hidden set), or `None` (clears the hidden set). It always sets `_visible_only = None` and NEVER touches `_order`. `on_bus_state` (`:501-511`) routes by namespace: the order namespace first, the visibility namespace `elif`.
- invariant (the property [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH] depends on): **the ORDER axis and the VISIBILITY axis have independent publishers and must not clobber each other.** A reorder published to `state_namespace` never changes the shown-set (a bare list touches only `_order`); a shown-map published to `visibility_namespace` never changes the order (`apply_visibility_state` passes `self._order` through unchanged). This is what lets [CXC-ICL-PANE-SWAP] drive a pane swap from a presence publisher on `console/instance/pane` while a human or an Intelligence concurrently reorders the rail on `_app/console/collab_order` — neither writer stomps the other. A pane swap folded into the order payload's `{hidden: [...]}` key would clobber any concurrent reorder, and vice versa; the engine provides two sources precisely so both can be used.
- requirement: a reimplementation MUST keep the two sources as two subscriptions with two independent apply paths; a publish on either namespace MUST leave the OTHER axis's state untouched. Exactly one widget should own each namespace ([DX-LEAF-OWNS-BEHAVIOR]); two writers to one shown-set namespace is a review reject.
- caveat (the uncoupling is per-AXIS, not per-SOURCE): the two sources are uncoupled on the order-vs-visibility axis, but they are NOT two independent visibility sources — both write the SAME `_hidden`/`_visible_only` fields. A `{hidden: [...]}` published on `state_namespace` overwrites whatever `visibility_namespace` last set, and `apply_visibility_state` clears any allow-list set through the order payload. A design that needs two concurrent visibility writers is NOT supported today.
- truthiness of a shown-map value (`_truthy`, `layout.py:514-517`): a string is shown iff it case-insensitively strips to one of `1`, `true`, `yes`, `on`, `shown`, `show`; any other value falls back to `bool(v)`. So `{"a": "no"}` HIDES `a`.
- default: `visibility_namespace` unset (`""`) → no second subscription is registered at all (`parser.py:1534`), and the shown-set is driven solely by the order payload.
- verified-by: `test_separate_visibility_namespace` (`:148-159`) — publishes a hidden-id LIST and then a `{id: bool}` shown-map on the second namespace and asserts both take effect; `test_parser_builds_ordered_stack_and_subscribes` (`:177-187`) asserts BOTH namespaces are subscribed for the same widget.
- scenario:
  - when: a rail declares `state_namespace: dash/order` and `visibility_namespace: dash/hidden`, and `["a"]` is published to `dash/hidden`
  - then: `a` is hidden and the rail's ORDER is unchanged; a subsequent `{"a": true, "c": false}` shown-map shows `a` and hides `c`
- see-also: [CXC-ORD-SHOWN-SET], [CXC-ICL-PANE-SWAP], [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ORDER]

### [CXC-ORD-REVERSIBLE] INVARIANT — the declared child list is NEVER mutated, so z-order stays declared and every change is reversible

- definition: no code path in `OrderedFlowContainer` reorders, inserts into, or removes from `self.children`. Order lives in `_order` (a list of ids) and visibility in `_hidden`/`_visible_only`; the declared tree is read-only to this container. Three consequences follow directly: (1) DRAW z-order and HIT-TEST order remain the DECLARED order (`Container.draw` `:201-203` and `hit_test_tree` `:214-216` iterate `self.children`), so overlapping children never flicker or change stacking when the layout order changes; (2) clearing the state restores the original layout exactly, because the original was never lost; (3) an external reader of the tree (the S4 personalization applier, a test, an Intelligence inspecting the scene) always sees the AUTHORED composition.
- requirement: a reimplementation MUST NOT mutate the declared child list to express order or visibility. Callers MUST treat the value returned by `_flow_children()` as READ-ONLY: on the identity path it is the live `self.children` list object, not a copy (`layout.py:404-405`), so mutating it would mutate the tree ([CXC-ORD-OPEN] O9).
- contrast (a different mechanism, deliberately): `intelligence/console/personalization.py :: apply_collaboration_selection` — the S4 LAUNCH-READ applier ([CXC-ICP-S4-DEFAULT-RAIL]) — DOES reorder `container.children` and write `child.visible` directly (`:300-310`). That is the persisted, launch-time curation path and is NOT this primitive; the two co-own `child.visible` at runtime ([CXC-ORD-OPEN] O5).
- verified-by: `test_declared_child_list_is_never_mutated` (`:162-174`) — publishes `{"order": ["c","b","a"], "hidden": ["a"]}`, asserts `[c.widget_id for c in rail.children] == ["a","b","c"]` unchanged, then clears both and asserts the laid order is `["a","b","c"]` again.
- scenario:
  - when: a reorder + hide is published, then the order is cleared with `null` and the hidden set with `{"hidden": []}`
  - then: the rail returns exactly to its declared layout — nothing was destroyed, so nothing must be rebuilt
- see-also: [CXC-ORD-CONTAINER], [CXC-ORD-SHOWN-SET], [CXC-R-ENGINE-HIT]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ORDER]

### [CXC-ORD-HOT-ADD] `register_added_child()` — a RUNTIME-added child appears immediately instead of being hidden by omission

- definition: `register_added_child(child)` (`layout.py:490-499`) is the hot-add hook. When an allow-list is active it ADDS the new id to `_visible_only`; when an explicit order is active it APPENDS the new id to `_order` (if absent); then it sets `child.visible = self._shown(child.widget_id)`. Without it, a widget dropped into a container under an active allow-list or order would be invisible or last-by-accident — hidden by OMISSION from a state that predates it. `RenderApp.add_widget_live` calls it after appending the child and before mounting (`runtime.py:946-953`, `isinstance(parent, OrderedFlowContainer)`); the console's launch-mount calls it duck-typed via `hasattr` (`personalization.py:355-356`).
- requirement: a container that carries an active order or allow-list MUST integrate a runtime-added child into BOTH (append to the order, admit to the allow-list) and MUST set its `visible` flag from the resolved shown-set, so a hot-added widget appears immediately ([CXC-ICP-REBIND] hot-add / live-subscribe — the D1 choice of [DEC-2026-07-25-FOLLOWUP-CALLS-SETTLED], built in the same W2 workstream as this container).
- caveat (stated, not corrected): the hook does NOT remove the new id from `_hidden`, so a hot-added child whose id is already in the hidden set stays hidden ([CXC-ORD-OPEN] O14). It also does not request a redraw — the caller damages the added subtree.
- default: with no order and no allow-list active, the hook only writes `child.visible` (which resolves to True unless the id is in `_hidden`).
- verified-by: exercised end-to-end (not asserted at the hook level) by `python/tests/render/test_hot_add.py` (its `_dash()` fixture is an `ordered_stack` with a `state_namespace`, `:34-40`) and by `python/tests/render/test_console_app_surfaces.py::test_s4_hot_add_mounts_into_running_rail_and_binds` (`:111-139`, a real `rail_col` `ordered_stack`); `python/tests/render/drive_ordered_reflow.py` is the visual proof driver for [SPEC:CXC-ORDER] + hot-add. **No test asserts the hook's own three effects directly** — see [CXC-ORD-VERIFY] ORD-C7.
- scenario:
  - when: `{"visible": ["a", "c"]}` is active and a widget `d` is hot-added via `add_widget_live`
  - then: `d` is admitted to the allow-list, appended to any active order, and shown immediately — not swallowed by the pre-existing allow-list
- see-also: [CXC-ORD-SHOWN-SET], [CXC-ICP-S4-DEFAULT-RAIL], [CXC-ICP-LAUNCH-BIND]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ORDER]

### [CXC-ORD-DAMAGE] INVARIANT — a redraw is requested ONLY on a real change, and the damage is scoped to this container

- definition: `on_bus_state` (`layout.py:501-511`) routes the delivery to the matching apply path, which returns whether the EFFECTIVE order or shown-set changed (`_commit`, `:476-488`, comparing the new order/hidden/visible against the current), and calls `self.request_redraw()` ONLY when it did. `Widget.request_redraw()` (`python/composeio/render/widget.py:169-180`) damages `self.arranged_rect` — this container's own region — not the whole surface; a container carries a mount context (`Container.on_mount`, `layout.py:151-153`) so the call is live. A SHRINK is covered because the runtime captures the pre-update arranged rect and damages old-UNION-new for the update's target (`runtime.py:1071-1092`).
- requirement: a reimplementation MUST gate the redraw on a real change — a delivery that resolves to the SAME effective order and shown-set MUST return without requesting a redraw — and MUST scope the damage to the container's own rect rather than escalating to a full-surface redraw. This is the container-level instance of [CXC-R-DAMAGE-ONCHANGE]: a widget MUST NOT rely on the transport to de-dupe, because retained-value replay at hot-add wire time, pattern fan-out, and whole-projection re-emits all re-deliver content the widget already reflects.
- verified-by: `test_reflow_requests_redraw_on_change` (`:190-202`) — asserts a real reorder sets `app._dirty`, and that an IDENTICAL republish does not. Note the honest limit of that second assertion: `Namespace.publish_state` (`python/composeio/namespace.py:77-85`) only notifies subscribers when `new != old`, so an identical republish never reaches `on_bus_state` at all and the test would pass even without the container's own gate. The gate's own coverage — a DIFFERENTLY-SHAPED payload resolving to the same effective state (e.g. `{"order": ["b","a","c"]}` after the list `["b","a","c"]`) — is established by inspection of `_commit`, not by a test ([CXC-ORD-VERIFY] ORD-C8).
- default: an unmatched namespace, or a payload that changes nothing, requests no redraw.
- scenario:
  - when: a rail's order state changes and the container reflows
  - then: exactly the rail's own region is damaged (old ∪ new), and the rest of the fidelity-rich console scene is not re-rastered
- see-also: [CXC-R-DAMAGE-ONCHANGE], [CXC-ORD-ORDER-STATE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ORDER]

