## 3. The declaration surface

### [CXC-ORD-DECLARE] Declaring one in `.compose.yaml` — three accepted type spellings, two namespace keys, flex keys otherwise

- definition: the parser registers `ordered_stack`, `orderedstack`, and `flow_stack` as container types (`parser.py:1473-1475`), all three constructing `OrderedFlowContainer` (`:1517-1531`). Keys read at construction: `direction` (through `_norm_direction`, so `vertical`/`horizontal` aliases apply), `align`, `justify`, `state_namespace` (**alias `order_namespace`**, first non-empty wins), and `visibility_namespace`. Every other container key (`id`, `gap`, `padding`, `width`, `align`, children, bindings, gestures, `state:`) is handled by the shared container path exactly as for a `stack`.
- subscriptions: a non-empty `state_namespace` appends `(namespace, node)` to `comp.subscriptions` (`:1532-1533`); a non-empty `visibility_namespace` appends a SECOND entry for the same widget (`:1534-1535`). An empty string registers nothing. Initial wiring does not replay a retained value (`runtime.py:747-756` gates replay on `_running`), so a scene-declared container holds the DECLARED order at boot unless the namespace carries an `initial:` seed or a producer publishes.
- requirement: a scene MUST declare the driving namespace in the scene's `namespaces:` block (so it is state-engine-typed and scoped) and MUST bind it to a container of one of the three registered types; a builder MUST NOT expect `visible:` attribute bindings to reflow a flex container (they set the flag but the child keeps its measured space — the reason [CXC-ICL-PANE-SWAP] uses `visibility_namespace` instead).
- default: `direction: column`, `align: start`, `justify: start`, both namespaces empty. A `direction`-less conversion from `stack` therefore CHANGES the axis ([CXC-ORD-OPEN] O1) — declare `direction:` explicitly.
- verified-by: `test_parser_builds_ordered_stack_and_subscribes` (`:177-187`) — asserts the parsed node is an `OrderedFlowContainer`, that both namespace keys landed on the instance, and that both namespaces appear in `comp.subscriptions` for that widget.
- scenario:
  - when: a scene declares `{type: ordered_stack, id: rail, direction: column, state_namespace: dash/order, visibility_namespace: dash/vis}`
  - then: the parser builds an `OrderedFlowContainer` and registers TWO subscriptions for it; publishing to either namespace reaches `on_bus_state` with that namespace name
- see-also: [CXC-ORD-CONTAINER], [CXC-ORD-PRODUCTION], [CXC-ORD-OPEN]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ORDER]

### [CXC-ORD-PRODUCTION] Production use — the console collaboration rail, and what is deliberately NOT yet wired

- definition: `intelligence/console/console.compose.yaml:1517-1523` declares the right-hand collaboration rail as `{type: ordered_stack, id: rail_col, direction: column, align: stretch, gap: 13, width: 332, state_namespace: _app/console/collab_order}`. The driving namespace is declared at `:1019-1022` as `{name: _app/console/collab_order, state_engine: json, export: true, scope: local, label: "Collaboration rail order/visibility"}` — `scope: local` (it never leaves the console) and NO `initial:` (so the rail boots in declared order). The scene comment records the conversion rationale: the reflow reaches EVERY card in the rail, not just the flex siblings a frozen absolute layout could touch, and with no state published the conversion is zero-diff.
- not-yet-wired (recorded as fact, NOT as a gap in this primitive): `visibility_namespace` and `console/instance/pane` appear NOWHERE in `console.compose.yaml` (zero hits across `intelligence/`). The second subscription is implemented, tested, and available; the console simply does not use it yet. Wiring it is the genuine future work owned by [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH] / [CXC-ICL-PANE-SWAP] (which adds the rail key, declares the namespace with its load-bearing `initial:`, and publishes the shown-map from the launch panel). Likewise the `order_namespace` alias and the `orderedstack` / `flow_stack` type spellings have ZERO usages in the repo.
- requirement: a builder wiring the pane swap MUST add `visibility_namespace` to the EXISTING `rail_col` declaration and MUST NOT fold the shown-map into `_app/console/collab_order` ([CXC-ORD-VIS-NAMESPACE]); no engine change, no parser change, and no new widget primitive is required, because this container already provides both sources.
- verified-by: `test_console_app_surfaces.py::test_s4_real_console_default_roster_is_the_four_scene_cards` (`:173-184`) exercises the real `rail_col` from the shipped scene; `test_console_render_idle.py:118` and `test_deliverable_console.py:648` also read `rail_col` from the live scene.
- scenario:
  - when: an Intelligence publishes `{"order": ["deliverable_card", "intelligence_card"], "hidden": ["away_watch_view"]}` to `_app/console/collab_order`
  - then: the console rail reorders and compacts in place, only the rail's region repaints, and the scene file is untouched — the change is reversible by publishing `null` + `{"hidden": []}`
- see-also: [CXC-ORD-DECLARE], [CXC-ICL-PANE-SWAP], [CXC-ICP-S4-DEFAULT-RAIL]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-ORDER]

