## 6. Capability facet

### [CAP-BINDABLE-ORDER] Bindable layout order + visibility — a live surface reorders/hides/reflows from a bus publish, reversibly

- kind: capability
- maturity: built
- tier-coverage: console (the shipped Intelligence Console rail), Linux-node / Python render engine (any `.compose.yaml` scene the Python engine renders, windowed or headless)
- not-covered: the embedded/MCU tiers — there is NO firmware implementation of `ordered_stack` (zero hits outside `python/`); a CYD/C3 surface cannot be reordered over the bus today
- verified-by: 9 passing tests in `python/tests/render/test_ordered_container.py` (identity/pixel-parity, list reorder, dict reorder+hide+compact, sticky visibility, allow-list, separate visibility namespace, non-mutation/reversibility, parser+subscriptions, change-gated damage), plus production use in `intelligence/console/console.compose.yaml:1517-1523`. Coverage gaps are named explicitly in [CXC-ORD-VERIFY] (ORD-C9, C10, C11, C12).
- depends-on: [CXC-ORD-CONTAINER]
- see-also: [SPEC:CXC-ORDER]
- flag: NOT yet added to [CAP-MAP]'s `see-also` roster in `49-capability-map.lts` — this task was scoped to ONE new document, so spec 49 was not edited. The `part-of: [CAP-MAP]` edge below makes the node reachable by the reverse-PART_OF walk and by `lts_find(kind=capability)` regardless; adding the roster line is a one-line follow-up for the architect.
- part-of: [CAP-MAP]

